A1. A professional bull tosser. ~ MATADOR
A7. A truly wild woman is a woman who gets this. ~ LOCKJAW
A13. Any animal with no feet. (This is no joke!) ~ APOD
A14. A grass product of Nevada. (This may be grass but it isn't green.) ~ WIDOW
A16. A gambling game. (Wild West stuff.) ~ FARO
A17. This comes soon after 9 A. M. in Tennessee. ~ TEN
A18. Petting recesses. ~ ALCOVES
A20. This clears cotton and confuses the mind. ~ GIN
A21. Exists. ~ IS
A22. The last half of a "year." ~ AR
A23. The source of that itchy underwear (Fem.). ~ EWE
A24. Flapper's favorite pronoun. ~ HE
A26. A sky pilot (abbr.). ~ DD
A27. Too many drivers think this is as good as a mile. ~ INCH
A29. Mamma's boys. ~ SONS
A31. A poet's "even." ~ EEN
A33. One of these in time saves the nine. ~ HIT
A35. Something a fat man doesn't have. ~ LAP
A36. "To avoid that run down feeling," keep out of the way of this. ~ CAR
A38. Some people get ill at this. ~ EASE
A40. This kind of hosiery gives a girl a run for her money. ~ CHEAP
A42. These are cooling. ~ FANS
A43. A dangerous thing to run. ~ RISK
A44. Something landlords and highwaymen do. ~ ROB
A45. Mah's last name. ~ JONG
A46. Where high society climbs even higher. ~ ALPS
A47. African golf balls. ~ BONES
A49. A garden where people grew everything but old. ~ EDEN
A51. Leap Year Idol (abbr.). (We apologize for that!) ~ LYI
A52. Pussyfoot. ~ PAW
A53. The science of technicalities. ~ LAW
A55. John Bull's beer. ~ ALE
A56. What all girls must be in order to go to heaven. ~ DEAD
A59. This is usually built to run on a bank. ~ MILL
A61. Manuscript (abbr.). ~ MS
A63. Like. ~ AS
A64. A choker. ~ BOA
A66. A feminine affirmative. ~ NO
A67. A conjunction. ~ OR
A68. A man may have this number of wives and still have one too many. ~ ONE
A70. This kind of hunter always finds what he's hunting. ~ TROUBLE
A73. The cause and effect of crosswords. ~ IRE
A74. That dumb girl. ~ DORA
A76. This is quite sufficient. ~ AMPLE
A77. Four of these make a pretty safe bet. ~ ACES
A78. The daughter of 23 horizontal. (Two words.) ~ EWELAMB
A79. This kind of a fellow sends his mother congratulations on his birthday. ~ EGOTIST

D1. A kind of idol. ~ MATINEE
D2. Anthropoids or quadrumanes. ~ APES
D3. A weight. ~ TON
D4. This is on the back of every Judge. (abbr.). ~ AD
D5. A noctural predatory arian sauropsidan of the coraciiform family. ~ OWL
D6. A bridal shower. ~ RICE
D7. Life is one darn thing after another; this is one darn fool after another. ~ LOVE
D8. Verb meaning to have a lot of unpaid bills. ~ OWE
D9. Knickered Females (abbr.). ~ KF
D10. There's more than one in a jug of moonshine. ~ JAG
D11. This is what the United States is. (Now you tell one.) ~ ARID
D12. The world's seven oddities. ~ WONDERS
D15. Direction of a famously warm climate. ~ DOWN
D18. This keeps people from being flat-footed. ~ ARCH
D19. Most women would rather do this than eat. (Not talk.) ~ SHOP
D22. A vague article. ~ AN
D25. Half of a printer's measure. ~ EN
D27. What it would be to kiss Auntie Everything. ~ INSIPID
D28. An unlawful interjection. ~ HIC
D29. This is both food and drink to a gold-digger. ~ SAP
D30. A dim light has a lot of this kind of power. ~ SCANDAL
D32. A well-known bird. ~ EARLY
D34. To cast. (As dice.) ~ THROW
D35. This covers a multitude of gins. ~ LABEL
D37. This is always flying up in the air. ~ ANGEL
D39. The more deadly suffix. ~ ESS
D41. A long time. (Even longer than Bryan has been running for things.) ~ EON
D42. Enemy. ~ FOE
D46. According to the style (Fr.). ~ ALAMODE
D47. There's a little bit of this in every good little girl. ~ BAD
D48. Our uncle. ~ SAM
D50. The best girl to take home is the girl who lives in this house. ~ NEAREST
D52. When this is shady it's usually kept dark. ~ PAST
D54. The first of a happy trio. ~ WINE
D57. Each (abbr.). ~ EA
D58. Musical food. ~ SOUP
D60. Behold. ~ LO
D62. Cold stuff. ~ SNOW
D64. A Russian persuader. ~ BOMB
D65. Capable. ~ ABLE
D67. These are very unrefined. ~ ORES
D69. Before. ~ ERE
D71. A strong kind of butter. ~ RAM
D72. One-half of a chorus girl's assets. ~ LEG
D73. Here (Fr.). ~ ICI
D75. What King Alfred's boy friends called him before he became great. ~ AL
D77. Preposition. ~ AT


